13. *Modulation into the Subdominant Duodene.*

Omit the top line *B¹*♭ + *D* + *F₁*♯ (or *To* + *Re* + *Fi*) of the duodene of *C* in scheme art. 11, and take a new line *G¹*♭ + *B*♭ + *D₁* (or *Sa* + *Ta* + *Ra*) at the bottom to obtain the duodene of *F* (or *Fa*). @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
The changes made are the reverse of those for modulations into the dominant. The notes *B¹*♭, *D* are depressed by a comma of 22 cents to *B*♭, *D₁*, and the extra note *F₁*♯ of the *C* duodene is raised by a diaskhisma of 20 cents to *G¹*♭ of the *F* duodene. @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
These changes are neglected in tempered intonation. But the most important change is that *B₁* (which is in the duodene, but not the decad of *F*) is depressed by a Semitone of 92 cents from *B₁* to *B*♭, and this being noticed in tempered music, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
becomes the distinguishing mark in the modulation of the major scale of *C* into that of the subdominant *F*.

14. *Modulation into the Mediant Duodene.*

@@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
Returning again to the duodene of *C*, we might omit the left column *D¹*♭ ± *A¹*♭ ± *E¹*♭ ± *B¹*♭ (or *ro* ± *lo* ± *mo* ± *to*) of the duodene of *C* ¶ (see scheme in art. 11) and introduce a new column on the right *C₂*♯ ± *G₂*♯ ± *D₂*♯ ± *A₂*♯ (or *de* ± *se* ± *ri* ± *ti*), @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
in which each new note is a *great diesis* of 42 cents lower than the note in the column omitted. This difference is ignored in playing tempered music, although the distinction is preserved in writing as *D*♭ and *C*♯, &c., but it is of great importance in just intonation. @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
This is termed modulation from the root *C* (or *Do*) into the *Mediant*, *E₁* (or *Mi*) as the root of the new duodene is the major Third or Mediant of the root *C* (or *Do*) of the old duodene.

15. *Modulation into the Minor Submediant Duodene.*

@@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
Similarly we might omit the right column *A₁* ± *E₁* ± *B₁* ± *F₁*♯ (or *la mi ti fi*) of the duodene of *C* (see scheme in art. 11) and introduce a new column *B²*♭♭ ± *F²*♭ ± *C²*♭ ± *G²*♭ (or *ta fu du su*) on the left, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
thus forming the duodene of *A¹*♭ (or *Lo*) or *minor submediant*.

16. *Modulation into the Relative and Correlative Duodenes.*

@@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
But it is usual to combine these two modulations with others into the subdominant of the mediant (that is the submediant) *A₁* (or *La*), generally called the *relative* on the one ¶ hand, and the dominant of the minor submediant, that is *E¹*♭ (or *Mo*), @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
sometimes called the *correlative*, on the other. In each case the root of the new duodene differs by a minor Third from the old root. The change for these two last modulations is considerable if we take the whole duodene into consideration, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
as may be seen by the schemes in art. 11, where the dotted lines mark off the new duodenes. But in the cases which occur in practice the changes are very small, especially when the difference of a comma is neglected as in tempered music. @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
The modulation into the relative is generally from the *major* scale of the decad of *C* or chords *F* + *A₁* − *C*, *C* + *E₁* − *G*, *G* + *B₁* − *D* into the *minor* scale of the relative decad of *A₁*, either consisting of the chords @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
*D₁* − *F* + *A₁*, *A₁* − *C* + *E₁*, *E₁* − *G* + *B₁* (in which there is only the use of *D₁* for *D*, to indicate this modulation), or else ¶ consisting of the chords *D₁* − *F* + *A₁*, *A₁* − *C* + *E₁*, *E₁* + *G₂*♯ − *B₁*, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
or even *D₁* + *F₂*♯ − *A₁*, *A₁* − *C* + *E*, *E₁* − *G₂*♯ − *B₁*, these being the three recognised forms of the minor scale. As the two latter forms are also acknowledged in tempered intonation (which, however, confuses *F₁*♯ and *F₂*♯), @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
the change of *G* into *G₂*♯ (or *so* into *se*, that is the sharpening of the Fifth by properly 70 cents) has generally been considered the mark of modulation from a major scale into its relative minor,—one of the commonest in music.

@@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
The modulation into the correlative is in the same way generally thought of as the change of the tonic major scale chords *F* + *A₁* − *C*, *C* + *E₁* − *G*, *G* + *B₁* − *D* (or *Fa* + *La* − *Do*, *Do* + *Mi* − *So*, *So* + *Ti* − *Re*) into the @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
tonic minor scale chords *F* − *A¹*♭ + *C*, *C* − *E¹*♭ + *G*, *G* − *B¹*♭ + *D* (or *Fa* − *Lo* + *Do*, *Do* − *Mo* + *So*, *So* − *To* + *Re*), in which however the chord *G* + *B₁* − *D* (or *So* + *Ti* − *Re*) may remain, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
and sometimes the chord *F* + *A₁* − *C* (or *Fa La Do*). This is not a modulation at all in the sense just explained, because there is no change of duodene or even decad. It is merely a change of scale within the same decad, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
that is, a new trichordal scale moving from 1 *C* ma.ma.ma. to 1 *C* mi.mi.mi., mi.mi.ma., or ma.mi.ma., art. 9. It would, however, be more consonant to ancient practice to restrict the term *modulation* to such changes of trichordal within the same duodene, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
and to use a new term for the more general operation.

17. *Duodenation.*

@@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
This is the term I propose to substitute for modulation when it means passing from one duodene to another which bears a known relation to the first. This relation may be very close, as in the cases just considered, or so remote that the two duodenes have only one note in common. @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
Thus the duodene of *D²*♭ and *C* have only the note *B¹*♭ in common. The annexed figure, called the *Duodenārium*, probably contains all such duodenations which occur even in modern music, though it is impossible to be certain how far the ambiguities @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
of tempered intonation may mislead the composer to consider as related, chords and scales which are really very far apart. It contains therefore an approximate estimate of 117, for the number of tones in an Octave which would be required to play in just intonation, @@G@@
>>>
<<<
@@G@@
===
and are roughly represented by the 12 tones of equal temperament.
